{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 37, :comments ["<b>This is the Torah of the burnt-offering, of the meal-offering, and of the sin-offering, and of the guilt-offering, and of the consecrations, and of the sacrifice of peace-offerings.</b> According to the order of the offerings in this verse you will find them arranged in sections: the section of the burnt-offering (עולה), then the section of the meal-offering (מנחה), and so with all the rest. And as for the fact that he inserted the consecrations (מלואים) between the guilt-offering (אשם) and the sacrifice of peace-offerings (זבח השלמים), when there is no consecration offering among the sections mentioned — if one should say that the intent refers to what is written earlier, 'take Aaron' (קח את אהרן), which is the section of consecrations, the order would not be correct. Rather, the matter is that he mentioned consecrations above, and with this the order will come correctly, for indeed a consecration offering is not a distinct offering like a sin-offering and a guilt-offering and the rest, but in the consecrations there are many offerings: sin-offering, burnt-offering, and peace-offering — and this is the language of 'consecrations' (מלואים) in the plural, and assuredly those offerings have already been mentioned." "<b>And behold,</b> this verse placed the burnt-offering first and sealed with the peace-offerings. It placed the burnt-offering first as is fitting, for it is the choicest and it is the most honored of all the offerings, and it sealed with the peace-offerings because it is fitting to seal with the quality of peace, which is the sustenance of the world. And our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: why are they called שלמים (peace-offerings)? Because they impose peace in the world. And you should know that the Name [of HaShem] was erased upon the waters in order to make peace between a man and his wife, and because the quality of peace is beloved, the Men of the Great Assembly enacted for us the conclusion of the prayer of the Eighteen [Benedictions] with the quality of peace, which is the blessing 'who blesses His people Israel with peace.' And so Solomon, peace be upon him, concluded the Song of Songs with this quality, as he said: אז הייתי בעיניו כמוצאת שלום — 'then was I in his eyes as one that found peace.' And our Rabbis of blessed memory said: even the beings on high need peace, as it is said (Job 25:2): המשל ופחד עמו עושה שלום במרומיו. And our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: זאת התורה לעולה למנחה ולחטאת ולאשם וגו׳ — whoever engages with the section of the burnt-offering is as if he offered a burnt-offering; with the section of the meal-offering, as if he offered a meal-offering; with the section of the sin-offering, as if he offered a sin-offering. And the meaning is that he reflects upon the explanation of the section, toward what matter it alludes, for through this the eyes of his intellect will be opened and he will understand wonders from the Torah of the offerings, and through this he will strive more in the fulfillment of the Torah and the commandments, and his sins will be forgiven him as though he had offered an offering — for one cannot say that the intent is that he should recite and review the language of the section in its bare verses without reflecting upon their interpretation. And similar to this they said: whoever says תהלה לדוד (Psalm 145) every day is assured that he is a son of the World to Come — the intent is that through his reflecting upon what the verses teach and to what they allude, he will then recognize and discern the wonders of 'perfect in knowledge' (תמים דעים), and his heart will be strengthened in faith in HaShem, blessed be He, and His service, and through this he will inherit the life of the World to Come. And for this reason our Sages of blessed memory enacted in the order of our prayer the mishnah of 'Which is their place [to say them]?' every morning, and they said concerning this: the Holy One, blessed be He, said: at the time when they recite them, I account it to them as if they had offered them before Me." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> זאת התורה לעולה למנחה ולחטאת ולאשם — great is Torah more than all the offerings, all of them, as it is said: זאת התורה לעולה למנחה ולחטאת ולאשם ולמלואים ולזבח השלמים. Whoever engages in Torah is as if he offered a burnt-offering, as if he offered a meal-offering, as if he offered a sin-offering, as if he offered a guilt-offering, and consecrations, and peace-offerings." "<b>And know</b> and understand that the categories of offerings are all nine in number — both the offerings that have a designated place, whose slaughter is in the north, and those whose slaughter is anywhere in the courtyard — and they are: burnt-offering (עולה), meal-offering (מנחה), sin-offering (חטאת), guilt-offering (אשם), thanksgiving-offering (תודה), peace-offering (שלמים), firstborn (בכור), tithe (מעשר), and Paschal offering (פסח). And you already know that the number nine is the culmination of the units and the culmination of all that can be grasped, and there is no grasp above nine. Therefore the offerings come in the count of nine to indicate that the intent of worship through the offerings is solely for the sake of the distinctive Name — and this is what they said in Torat Kohanim: that in the Torah, regarding the matter of the offerings, one does not find אל nor אלהים nor אלהיך, but only YHWH, the distinctive Name, so as not to give an opening to a disputant to argue. And further they said: אשה ריח ניחוח — this empties all the forms of worship [of any intent but] for the sake of the distinctive Name. And the offering was brought near upon the altar, and the altar's height was ten cubits, as it is written in Chronicles (II Chronicles 4:1): ועשר אמות קומתו — 'and ten cubits its height.' And what the Torah writes, ושלש אמות קומתו — 'and three cubits its height' — is the place of the fire-pile alone [from the surrounding ledge and above]. And this altar's place is precisely designated and set apart in the Holy Temple, and it is forbidden to move it from its place forever, as it is written (II Chronicles 22:1): וזה מזבח לעולה לישראל. And it is the place from which Adam was created, as our Rabbis of blessed memory mentioned: 'Adam was created from the place of his atonement.' And it is the place where Adam the first man offered, and Cain and Abel his sons, and there Noah built the altar for the burnt-offering, and there Abraham built the altar, and there our father Isaac was bound. You find thereby that this place is set apart for the offerings of all the righteous and all the pious of the generations. And since this place is set apart for all the categories of offerings, which are nine, you will find regarding the three Patriarchs — Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob — that the word מקום is alluded to nine times. In the section of the Binding (Akedah) you will find מקום four times (Genesis 22:3ff.): ויקם וילך אל המקום, וירא את המקום, ויבאו אל המקום, ויקרא אברהם שם המקום ההוא — these are Abraham and Isaac. And you will find regarding Jacob (Genesis 28): ויפגע במקום, ויקח מאבני המקום, וישכב במקום, אכן יש ה׳ במקום הזה, מה נורא המקום הזה, ויקרא את שם המקום ההוא בית אל. And ויפגע במקום is not counted in the number, for it does not speak of a physical thing, but rather that he encountered the Shekhinah — and behold, between all of them they are nine. And when the Holy Temple was destroyed, this place was swallowed up, as Jeremiah mentioned (Lamentations 2:9): טבעו בארץ שעריה — 'they have sunk into the ground her gates.' And the letter tet of טבעו is written small, alluding to the swallowing of the place that is alluded to in the Torah nine times, and to the absence of the offerings, which are nine categories."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {3 "Rabbeinu Bahya cites II Chronicles 22:1 for the verse וזה מזבח לעולה לישראל, but this verse appears at II Chronicles 8:12 (or I Chronicles 22:1 in different numbering); the citation as printed appears corrupt. The small tet in טבעו (Lamentations 2:9) is a masoretic tradition Rabbeinu Bahya reads as a numeric allusion (tet = 9)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:24:13.969594844Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}